\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

Currently, a few integer metaprogramming utilities feel distinctly lacking
from the C++ standard library.
This includes utilities for classifying integers into standard or extended integer types,
utilities for obtaining integers of at least a certain bit width, and more.

This proposals acts as an omnibus for such utilities.

\h2{Integer classification}

C++ has a long-standing taxonomy of integer types,
but no means of classifying integers according to that taxonomy:

\pre{
integral types
   ├── signed or unsigned integer types
   │   ├── standard integers: \tcode{int}, \tcode{unsigned}, etc.
   │   ├── extended integers: \tcode{__int128} etc.
   │   └── P3666 bit-precise integers: (\tcode{unsigned}) \tcode{_BitInt(N)}
   ├── character types: \tcode{char}, \tcode{wchar_t}, etc.
   └── \tcode{bool}
}

\tcode{std::is_integral_v} covers the integral types,
and \tcode{std::is_signed_v} and \tcode{std::is_unsigned_v} allow classifying arithmetic types
as being signed or unsigned,
but other useful classifications are not available.

\h3(id="classification-traits-motivation"){Motivation}

Being able to classify integers into these more specific categories
is mostly useful for constraining templates.

\Bex{
A function wanting to accept any character type
(for the purposes of forming \tcode{std::string_view} etc.)
would be constrained using \tcode{std::is_character_v<T>}.

A function wanting to accept only standard integer types
(to avoid opting into an unbounded set which includes \tcode{__int128}, \tcode{_BitInt(N)}, etc.)
should be constrained using \tcode{std::is_standard_integer_v<T>}.
}

\h3{Design}

To address this problem,
I propose the following type traits:
\cppblock{
// true if T is a standard integer type
template<class T> struct is_standard_integer;
template<class T> inline constexpr bool is_standard_integer_v =
  is_standard_integer<T>::value;

// true if T is an extended integer type
template<class T> struct is_extended_integer;
template<class T> inline constexpr bool is_extended_integer_v =
  is_extended_integer<T>::value;

// true if T is a bit-precise integer type
// (conditional on P3666 being accepted)
template<class T> struct is_bit_precise_integer;
template<class T> inline constexpr bool is_bit_precise_integer_v =
  is_bit_precise_integer<T>::value;

// true if T is one of char, wchar_t, char8_t, char16_t, or char32_t
template<class T> struct is_character;
template<class T> inline constexpr bool is_character_v =
  is_character<T>::value;
}

\h2{Minimum-width integer alias templates}

It is commonly useful to obtain an integer type of at least a certain bit width.

\h3(id="minimum-width-aliases-motivation"){Motivation}

\Bex{
In multiprecision arithmetic,
one often wants an integer of at least double the bit width of another integer type,
such as to hold the result of multiplying two integers of that type.
Making functions such as the following generic is hard though:
\cppblock{
std::uint64_t widening_mul(std::uint32_t x, std::uint32_t y) {
  return std::uint64_t(x) * std::uint64_t(y);
}
}
How does on generically obtain an integer of double the width?
Simply returning a \tcode{struct} containing two integers of the operand type
and never attempting to spell the wider integer type
does not solve the problem of performing an \q{intrinsic} \tcode{*} operation.
If the compiler has a wider-type, we do want to use it.

This also causes an issue for the 64-bit case:
\cppblock{
unsigned __int128 widening_mul(std::uint64_t x, std::uint64_t y) {
  return static_cast<unsigned __int128>(x) * static_cast<unsigned __int128>(y);
}
}
Not every compiler supports \tcode{unsigned __int128},
and even if it does exist, we have to use a non-portable spelling of it.
}

\Bex{
In modular arithmetic, we also commonly want an integer with double the width:
\cppblock{
std::uint32_t modular_mul(std::uint32_t x, std::uint32_t y, std::uint32_t mod) {
  return std::uint32_t(std::uint64_t(x) * std::uint64_t(y) % mod);
}
}
Without first converting to \tcode{std::uint64_t},
the multiplication could wrap
(which is effectively an operation modulo \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mn{32}}})
and yield an incorrect result.

Such modular arithmetic is used in e.g. \tcode{std::linear_congruential_engine}.
}

\h3(id="minimum-width-aliases-design"){Design}

I propose alias templates in \header{type_traits}
that allow obtaining an integer type of at least a certain bit width:

\cppblock{
template<std::size_t N> struct int_least;
template<std::size_t N> using int_least_t = int_least<N>::type;
template<std::size_t N> struct uint_least;
template<std::size_t N> using uint_least_t = uint_least<N>::type;

template<std::size_t N> struct int_fast;
template<std::size_t N> using int_fast_t = int_fast<N>::type;
template<std::size_t N> struct uint_fast;
template<std::size_t N> using uint_fast_t = uint_fast<N>::type;
}

The crucial difference between \tcode{int_least_t} and \tcode{int_fast_t}
is that the former optimizes for integer width,
while the latter optimizes for speed.

\Bex{
On a 32-bit platform,
\tcode{std::int_least_t<24>} might be a quirky 24-bit signed integer type with 8 bits of padding,
whereas \tcode{std::int_fast_t<24>} should be a 32-bit signed integer type.
}

\h4{Consistency with existing aliases}

For values of \tcode{N} for which there already exists a \tcode{std::int_leastN_t} alias,
the alias templates should be equivalent to the existing alias.
Otherwise, the inconsistency would be confusing and surprising.
It is also unclear what benefit there might be to defining \tcode{std::int_least_t<32>}
to be different from \tcode{std::int_least32_t}.

\h4{Usage examples}

\Bex{
Firstly, \tcode{std::uint_least_t<128>}
would be a portable spelling of an 128-bit unsigned integer type,
if such a type is provided.
This is already useful for silencing \tt{-Wpedantic} warnings
about \tcode{unsigned __int128} being a non-standard extension.
}

\Bex{
Secondly, a generic version of the \tcode{widening_mul} function could be written as follows:
\cppblock{
template<class T>
std::uint_fast_t<2 * std::numeric_limits<T>::digits> widening_mul(T x, T y) {
  return static_cast<std::uint_fast_t<2 * std::numeric_limits<T>::digits>>(x)
       * static_cast<std::uint_fast_t<2 * std::numeric_limits<T>::digits>>(y);
}
}
Of course, since \tcode{std::uint_fast_t<2 * ...>} might not be defined for all \tcode{T},
it would be necessary to have a fallback solution returning a \tcode{struct} for those cases.
}

\h2{Arithmetic width}

Querying the width of integer types is cumbersome at least,
and in the case of floating-point types, not directly possible.
\ul{
  \li{
    For an integer type \tcode{T}, the width is
    \tcode{std::numeric_limits<std::make_unsigned_t<T>>::digits}.
    The \tcode{std::make_unsigned_t} is necessary because
    \tcode{std::numeric_limits<std::int32_t>::digits} is 31, not 32.
  }
  \li{
    For a floating-point type \tcode{T},
    there isn't really a way to obtain the \q{width}.
    That is, the amount of bits part of actually representing the value.
    \tcode{sizeof(T) * CHAR_BIT} can sometimes be used,
    but reports the wrong result for e.g. x87 \tcode{long double},
    which has an 80-bit representation but is padded to 128 bits.
  }
}

\h3(id="arithmetic-width-design"){Design}

I propose a \tcode{std::width} type trait in \header{type_traits}:
\cppblock{
template<class T> struct width;
template<class T> inline constexpr std::size_t width_v = width<T>::value;
}

This trait is specialized for all arithmetic types
and yields the number of bits in the value representation of the type.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5046").

